# `[0, 1].flatMap(pick)` takes the elements out of whatever the function answers with that is a list. Where that may be a list that nobody follows, the new list may hold anything.
#   const rows = JSON.parse("[[1,2],[3]]");
#   function Rows(i) { return rows[i]; }
#   function pick(i) { return new Rows(i); }
#   function followed(i) { return ["a", "b"]; }
#   function made(i) { return { x: "a" }; }
#   function either(i) { switch (i) { case 1: return { y: "b" }; case 2: return { z: "c" }; case 3: return { u: "d" }; case 4: return { v: "e" }; default: return { w: "f" }; } }
#   [0, 1].flatMap(pick)[0];  [0, 1].flatMap(followed)[0];  [0, 1].flatMap(made)[0];  [0, 1].flatMap(either)[0];
# What a plain function returns to `new` counts only if it is an object: of `anything` the objects that nobody has seen are left, without the primitives.
# While the row looked into the lists that have a site and into nothing else, the first read was proven to give undefined or an object, and `typeof v === "number"` behind it was folded away: 900 | 0.
# The other three say that the first is not empty-handed: the elements of a list in sight are followed, and an object of the program that is no list is stored as it is.
# Five strangers meet in what `either` answers with, one more than a set takes, so the last of the three stores a lost value: no layout of the program is written below Array, so it is no list.
